<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">White movement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as White Army, White Guard, Whites (Russian Civil War)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The White movement was the loose coalition of anti-Bolshevik forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>. Led mainly by former tsarist officers such as Kornilov, Denikin, Kolchak, Yudenich and Wrangel, and supported at times by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> hosts, its armies fought from the Don and Kuban, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> and the Baltic. Divided in its aims and hostile to national independence movements, it was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, and its last forces left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> in 1920.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/white-movement.jpg" alt="White movement" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Personal_convoy_of_May-Mayevsky_dances_lezginka(1919).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Russian Revolution they fought the <b>White movement</b>, above all in the Makhnovshchina in Ukraine.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/anarchism">Anarchism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Russian Civil War it served as an unofficial anthem of Admiral Kolchak's <b>White Army</b>, while its earliest known performance by communist troops dates from 1918.</blockquote><a href="/songs/farewell-of-slavianka">Farewell of Slavianka</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;The Red Army Is the Strongest&quot;, widely known by its opening words as &quot;<b>White Army</b>, Black Baron&quot;, is a Soviet march with words by Pavel Gorinshtein and music by Samuel Pokrass.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-red-army-is-the-strongest">The Red Army Is the Strongest</a></li><li><blockquote>It allied with the <b>Whites</b> at times while negotiating with the Bolsheviks.</blockquote><a href="/state/alash-autonomy">Alash Autonomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Disappointed by the <b>Whites</b>' refusal of autonomy, Validi negotiated with Moscow, and in February 1919 the Bashkir army went over to the Red side.</blockquote><a href="/state/bashkurdistan">Bashkurdistan</a></li><li><blockquote>The coast passed to the <b>Whites</b> later in 1918, and Soviet rule returned only in 1920.</blockquote><a href="/state/black-sea-soviet-republic">Black Sea Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
